#--: Overview

#~"z" is the unit that says what a build is. The compiler #b:"writes" it,
before the build begins, from the values the build uses after every flag:
the target, the unit, the switches. A program reads them as ordinary
constants of an ordinary unit:

#code language=zerolang: """
  main: function is {
      if z.build.os.linux then print "on linux"
      print "built as \{z.build.main} for \{z.build.triple}"
  }
"""

There is no answer machinery behind it and nothing native about it: the
values are constants written into a file, so a unit-level #~"if" and a
#~"require:" block fold on them like on any other constant, and a branch
they decide never reaches the C.

#code language=zerolang: """
  require: unit {
      library: if z.build.os == z.build.oskind.windows then {
          "ws2_32"
      } else {
          ""
      }
  }
"""

#--: Where it lives

The files are generated into the compiler's own folder, #~".zerolang/",
which sits beside a project's #~"project.z" (see #~"zc.pdoc"). Outside a
project it is a folder of the user's cache directory
(#~"$XDG_CACHE_HOME/zerolang/", else #~"~/.cache/zerolang/"), so nothing is
ever written beside a lone source file. Each target has a folder of its
own, so builds for several targets sit side by side:

#code: """
  .zerolang/
    .gitignore                    -- `*`: the folder is disposable
    targets/x86_64-linux/
      z.z                         -- publishes `build` and `project`
      z/build.z                   -- the build's values
"""

#~"z/project.z" is not a file of that folder: the project's own
#~"project.z" is #b:"mounted" there, alone, so #~"z.project.main" is the
#~"main" the project file sets. A file is rewritten only when its text
changes, and the build reads the text it generated, not the copy on disk,
so two builds running at once never read each other's values.

The generated layer is bound #b:"between" the source roots and the
stdlib: a project's own unit named #~"z" shadows it, exactly as a user unit
shadows the stdlib's.

#--: z.build

#ul:
  [#*: #~"os" -- the operating system the build is for, an #~"oskind"]
  [#*: #~"arch" -- the architecture the build is for, an #~"archkind"]
  [#*: #~"triple" -- the #~"--target" triple as given (or the project's
    #~"target"), else the host's, as #~"<arch>-<os>"]
  [#*: #~"main" -- the unit the build compiles]
  [#*: #~"sourceDir" -- the first source root, relative to the project
    directory]
  [#*: #~"outputDir" -- where the executable goes when #~"-o" names none]
  [#*: #~"release", #~"fastHash", #~"readableNames", #~"eager" -- the
    switches, #~"true" or #~"false"]
  [#*: #~"zcVersion" -- the version of #~"zc" that wrote the file]

#ul:
  [#*: #~"oskind" -- #~"variant { linux: null  darwin: null  windows: null  other: null }"]
  [#*: #~"archkind" -- #~"variant { x86_64: null  aarch64: null  other: null }"]

#~"value.arm" is always a predicate, so #~"z.build.os.linux" and
#~"z.build.os == z.build.oskind.linux" fold alike.

No value names an absolute path: a path is relative to the project
directory, so the file has the same shape on every machine. The header
comment lays out where each root of the build comes from, and names the C
toolchain the build used -- in the comment only. The toolchain is the C
backend's business, and no constant a program can read names it.

#p: #b:"Caveat"

#~"os" and #~"arch" come from #~"--target" (or the project's #~"target")
when one is given and from the host otherwise. A cross #~"--cc" used
#b:"without" #~"--target" therefore leaves them describing the host while
the C compiler targets something else.

The triple is #b:"parsed", not substring-matched: each #~"-" separated
component is matched whole against the arm names and their aliases, and a
triple naming no operating system or two of them is refused at the flag.
See the cross-compilation section of #~"zc.pdoc" for the vocabulary.

#--: z.project

The project's own #~"project.z", when there is one: #~"z.project.sourceDir",
#~"z.project.main", and every other setting the file gives, as written. A
setting the file leaves out is not a member.
